# Rules for Windows PowerShell Environment
- On Windows/PowerShell, NEVER wrap commands in `cmd.exe /c`. Run them natively in PowerShell.
- If a long-running command (cargo, npm, docker) shows "completion could not be observed", do NOT retry. Wait or read the output file.

## Terminal & Loop Prevention

- "Command completion could not be observed" means the command DID run. The terminal content block shown IS the real output. Treat it as successful. Do NOT re-run the command.
- Maximum 2 reads of any single file per task. If the content looks the same as a previous read, the file hasn't changed — stop.
- For builds: run `cargo build -p effectcraft 2>&1 | Out-File build.log` once. Read build.log once. If it ends with "Finished", the build succeeded.
- If you find yourself about to repeat an action you already performed, STOP and tell the user what's blocking you instead of retrying.
- Limit codebase searches to at most 2 before starting edits. If you already know the file path, read it directly.
